  "name": "BETA 2 TRANSFERRIN CARBOHYDRATE RESISTANT BETA TRACE PROTEIN",
  "code": "1039",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"195\" data-end=\"408\">The <strong data-start=\"199\" data-end=\"221\">Beta-2 Transferrin</strong> and <strong data-start=\"226\" data-end=\"280\">Carbohydrate-Resistant Beta Trace Protein (CR-BTP)</strong> tests are highly specific markers for detecting the presence of <strong data-start=\"345\" data-end=\"374\">cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)</strong> in nasal, ear, or wound drainage.</p>\n<p data-start=\"410\" data-end=\"436\">These tests are used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"437\" data-end=\"804\">\n<li data-start=\"437\" data-end=\"509\">\n<p data-start=\"439\" data-end=\"509\">Confirm <strong data-start=\"447\" data-end=\"460\">CSF leaks</strong>, such as in <strong data-start=\"473\" data-end=\"491\">CSF rhinorrhea</strong> or <strong data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"507\">otorrhea</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"510\" data-end=\"574\">\n<p data-start=\"512\" data-end=\"574\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"521\" data-end=\"539\">post-traumatic</strong> or <strong data-start=\"543\" data-end=\"560\">post-surgical</strong> CSF leakage</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"575\" data-end=\"710\">\n<p data-start=\"577\" data-end=\"710\">Differentiate <strong data-start=\"591\" data-end=\"598\">CSF</strong> from other body fluids like serum, mucus, or tears, which do not contain these markers in significant amounts</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"711\" data-end=\"804\">\n<p data-start=\"713\" data-end=\"804\">Guide <strong data-start=\"719\" data-end=\"747\">surgical decision-making</strong> and reduce the risk of complications like <strong data-start=\"790\" data-end=\"804\">meningitis</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"790\" data-end=\"804\">If Positive for Beta-2 Transferrin or CR-BTP (Elevated/Detected)</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CSF Rhinorrhea</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates CSF is leaking into the nasal passages&mdash;typically after trauma, surgery, or spontaneously</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CSF Otorrhea</strong></td>\n<td>Suggests CSF is leaking into the ear canal, often due to temporal bone fractures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Post-surgical CSF Leak</strong></td>\n<td>Helps confirm presence of CSF following neurosurgical or ENT procedures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>These proteins are absent in blood, tears, and mucus, making them highly specific for CSF. Their detection confirms that the fluid is of cerebrospinal origin.</p>",
